/*
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    integrativebioinformatics/pulposeq Nextflow config file for running minimal tests
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    Defines input files and everything required to run a fast and simple pipeline test.

    Sized from an observed full run on the chr1 test dataset, using the % allocated
    figures described at https://docs.seqera.io/nextflow/tutorials/metrics -- that is,
    peak RSS against the memory directive and raw %CPU against cpus * 100. Memory is in
    binary units, matching Nextflow's own .GB.

        label                peak RSS  % mem   peak %CPU  % cpu   longest  % time
        process_single         3.4 GB    68%        94%*    94%    4.1 min    1.7%
        process_low            1.4 GB    47%       100%     50%    0.6 min    0.2%
        process_medium         8.2 GB    55%       265%     33%   15.1 min    6.3%
        process_high          10.9 GB    43%       394%     98%   84.2 min   35.1%
        process_high_memory   17.4 GB    43%       145%     36%   23.8 min    3.3%

        * GFFCOMPARE on a single core; RENDER_REPORT, the memory driver, reached 39%.

    Sizing notes:

      - MINIMAP2_ALIGN (process_high) is the only process close to its CPU allocation,
        so its 4 cores are kept. It is also the longest task at 84 minutes, hence 3 h
        rather than a tighter limit.
      - CHOPPER (process_medium) used 15% of eight cores. It runs as
        `zcat | chopper | gzip` and is bound by single-threaded compression, not thread
        count, so the allocation drops to four -- still ample for NANOCOMP, which is the
        label's real CPU driver at 265%.
      - BAMBU (process_high_memory) reached 145% of four cores and 43% of 40 GB, so both
        are reduced.
      - process_single keeps 5 GB: at 68%, RENDER_REPORT is the tightest fit in the run
        and has the least room to spare.

    Memory targets leave roughly 30-40% headroom over observed peaks. Judging memory by
    peak RSS rather than peak vmem is deliberate: RENDER_REPORT reports over 1 TB of
    virtual memory, which is address space R and Quarto reserve without touching.

    These are test-dataset figures. Real datasets, and whole-genome annotations in the
    metadata refinement steps, need the light / medium / large profiles instead.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
*/

process {
    // Ceiling applied after the task.attempt multiplier. Must stay >= the largest
    // request below, otherwise it silently clamps the labels and the numbers in this
    // file stop meaning anything. Lower it to your node size for a real cap.
    resourceLimits = [ cpus: 4, memory: 24.GB, time: 3.h ]

    withLabel:process_single {
        cpus   = 1
        memory = 5.GB
        time   = 1.h
    }
    withLabel:process_low {
        cpus   = 2
        memory = 3.GB
        time   = 1.h
    }
    withLabel:process_medium {
        cpus   = 4
        memory = 12.GB
        time   = 1.h
    }
    withLabel:process_high {
        cpus   = 4
        memory = 15.GB
        time   = 3.h
    }
    withLabel:process_high_memory {
        cpus   = 2
        memory = 24.GB
        time   = 1.h
    }
}
